Subject: Ann Landers comments on sexually explicit email
Date: 16 Mar 1994 17:16:59 GMT
Message-ID: <2m7eub$4th@bigfoot.wustl.edu>

An Ann Landers column in today's St. Louis Post-Dispatch included
a letter from a teenager who had discovered that his/her 12-year-old
sister was receiving sexually explicit email from a college student pen pal.
The teenager wanted advise on whether to confront the sister, tell
the parents, etc.  After answering the question, Ann Landers said,
"According to Chicago attorney Joan Hall, there is no federal statute that 
prohibits sending pornography by computer.  It's too bad the law hasn't
caught up with the technology."

I called the post office, and (no surprise here) there is no law
that prohibits sending pornography through the postal mail either.
The postal worker I spoke with said the only restrictions she could
find were contained in Section 30.10(a) of the postal code.  These
restrictions required sexually-oriented advertisements to be marked.
The also provided a means for postal customers to request that they
not receive these advertisements.

Just another example of people blaming the technology.


