In article <jdd.763690586@cdf.toronto.edu>,
John DiMarco <jdd@cdf.toronto.edu> wrote:
> Well, since universities own these systems, they presumably have the right
> to dictate how they should be used. 
> I'm not a big fan of totalitarian policies, but we do place some restrictions
> on our users (eg. we don't give them root access).  

Point missed. Of course they have the *right* to do this. My question
is whether such action is *reasonable*.

And about "we don't give them root", well, that is the obvious answer,
but I see it from this point: Users on a system should be allowed
*everything* that is not damaging to other users or overall systems.
Every restriction should be properly justified. Thus you don't even
get into the problem of defining what's "necessary" for a user.

Dont't try to enforce arbitrary restrictions like "gopher yes, www no"
[an actual case] since such restrictions won't help anybody. Don't
give ordinary users root since they could wreak havoc (even
unintentionally). That is a clear distinction, IMHO.

Olaf
