Subject: Advertising Sexually Explicit material
Message-ID: <5607@bsu-cs.bsu.edu>
Date: 16 Mar 94 18:59:30 GMT

Good people on the INTERNET,

I got a piece of "junk mail" yesterday that disturbs me. I am sharing it
with you all here for your consideration. I am concerned about it for
two reasons:

1) I believe that information should be FREE and the NET should be free
of censorship.

2) I ALSO believe that as users of the net we have rights to PRIVACY and
that should include freedom from unsolicited vendor information.

THOSE are the primary issues. Subordinant to those is another issue that
I also want to mention. As an educator and advocate for the use of
information resources in th schools and classrooms I am VERY DISTURBED
that solicitations of for sexually explicit material should be broadcast
out to a MAILING LIST on the net. I have NO IDEA how I got onto this
mailing list and I am VERY CONCERNED that whatever e-strategy the
creators of the list used may also snare students in schools whose
parents and communities may NOT APPROVE of the materials offered.

Hey... I am NOT a prude and I do NOT SUGGEST that we start censoring
INTERNET TRAFFIC to keep the smut out! *ell's Bells! That would take out
most of the notesfiles in NETNEWS! NO WAY!

HOWEVER, I do think that there is a need for us all to be cautious
because the guardians against change or innovation in the educational
system will look for ANY EXCUSE to tell us: "NO! You can't bring
INTERNET CONNECTIVITY into OUR SCHOOL!" 

Speaking only as one teacher who helps other teachers learn about USING
TECHNOLOGIES IN THE SCHOOLS... I'm archiving the explicit ad and using
it as an example in my telecommunications classes. The point I try to
make to the teachers that I work with is that we need to EDUCATE our
students about ALL THE POSSIBILITIES regarding information on the NET
and help them learn to be discriminating consumers of information. I do
NOT think it is our job to limit the opportunities available to our
students; but rather to help them become responsible and intellignet
consumers of information.

Also... isn't there a NETWORK issue here with crossing the lines between

commercial and educational uses of the network? I thought there were
safeguards against this stuff? How do we find out what those are? 

That is why I am posting this note. I fully expect to be flamed by
persons thinking I am taking an unreasonably conservative position.
(SIGH) The Irony of it all. Oh well... I have an asbestos UNIX account.

Sincerely yours,

Bob Blomeyer
Co-Chair, American Educational Research Association
Electronic Networking Special Interest Group
Teachers College
Ball State University
Muncie, Indiana

