I will only address the case of universities.

The mission of university is the create, preserve, and transmit
knowledge. I believe that experience shows that the best way for a
university to work toward this mission is to promote and protect free
expression.  When cost effective, it should provide free speech forums
for students, faculty, and staff. 

Examples of traditional cost-effective forums include use of outdoor
space as a forum (this use has low incremental cost), student
newspapers (typically supported via fees, subscriptions, and
advertising), after hour use of classrooms and auditoriums (low
incremential cost.) At my school, student computer use is supported by
several computer fees and general funds. The incremental cost of an
offensive articles is much lower that the cost of censoring such
articles.

Because a censorship system would 1) work against the university's
mission and 2) cost more than a free expression scheme, a censorship
scheme is unwise.

