	Well, I first may as well give a little information about our
system. We're a student-run and operated cluster offering unlimited (within
the confines of our hardware) Internet access to about 7500+ students,
faculty and staff. We've been around for almost six years now, and have a
fairly decent variety of users -- a large number who just use the cluster
for email, but quite a few who use it for a lot more.

	We are the only real place on campus that anyone can have completely
unrestricted access to computing facilities and software. We're usually
among the first on campus to have available most of the publicly available
software out there and offer a good enviroment, for programmers,
sophisticated users, people who want to play games, budding sysadmins, etc.

	Anyhow, here's the general idea on how our policies work. Hope you
find something that works for you.

: 1.  Accounts that have a guessable password.

	The best way to deal with this is to turn the account off, and tell
them to physically come in to have it turned back on. If YOU can crack these
passwords, then you can bet that a lot of other people can as well. It's
better to have to deal with the hassle of making someone come down to your
office and get a standard "good-password" speech than to have your system
messed up by some h0zer.

: 2.  Accounts with multiple off-site logins.

	This depends on the situation -- our site has about 7500 users, most
of whom are students. Very few use off-site accounts, but if we see people
who do, we generally have a staff member send a 'talk' to them to see what
they are doing -- who have quite a few users who are off at other schools
for a semester or two. There are also a fair number who use the account from
outside the school after graduation, until their accounts go away. Just
talking to users from the outside, though, shows us quite a few accounts
that are either shared or possibly cracked.

: 3.  Accounts that have large mail boxes which are impacting the system.

	This is something we have a chronic problem with. What we generally
do if the problem gets truly critical is to gzip their mail spools and plop
them in their home directories. We generally don't do this unless we have
problems with our mail server filling up, which happens every so often.

	We do have disk quotas on the accounts, although they're more
flexible than on most other systems, but, unlike many other systems, mail
spool usage counts against the quota.

: 4.  Accounts running password guessers, COPS or TOPS.

	CRUSH. Those accounts are generally shut off. They are reactivated
only after a talk with the General Manager and Site Manager (gotta love
these titles...), and then only if they ascertain there was no malicious
intent. If it happens again, the accounts are gone.

: 5.  Accounts that are harrassing other users.

	Generally, we give a warning to the users in question, and have them
discuss it with the GM/SM and/or the Board of Directory (all elected student
positions) -- multiple offenses, though, are subject to account termination.

: 6.  Accounts that are trying to or have defeated security.

	Depending on the situation, see #4 above.

: 7.  Accounts that breakin to other machines or accounts.

	Account is deactivated, pending a talk with the perpetrator. If this
recurs or is a particularly malicious thing, the account is terminated.

: 8.  Printing of pornographic pictures/gifs.

	In general, what users keep in their accounts is their business,
unless it (a) violates disk quotas severely or (b) is illegal, copyrighted
materials such as warez of various sorts. 

: 9.  Other incidents.  Please describe.

	Well, this is all on a case-by-case basis. The general idea, though,
is that people will make mistakes, and will often some do silly and/or
stupid things. We try to maintain an environment that is as free and
unrestricted as possible, and we allow mistakes. What we do NOT allow are
breaches of the law, commonly-accepted netiquette, or serious security
violations, and we will not hesitate to crush people if it becomes
necessary, or to take matters to higher authorities (University Office of
Student Conduct, the police, etc.)

