I'm not a sysadm, just a poor user, but I'd like to reply to the questions
anyway. The answers apply to the computer science lab of university of Pisa,
Italy.

> 1.  Accounts that have a guessable password.

Nobody checks (apparently). The password file isn't readable by normal users.
 
> 2.  Accounts with multiple off-site logins.

Not possible. Students are kept off from Internet; they can only use
E-Mail, news, gopher and mosaic. Common things like talk, ftp and telnet
to/from outside hosts are forbidden - and made impossible thanks to firewalls
(actually I don't know how much of this 'protection' is really useful: for
example it's possible to do anonymous ftp through mail or gopher...).

Researchers and teachers can use the net, instead. Students and researchers/teachers share the same computing center, but not the same hosts.

> 3.  Accounts that have large mail boxes which are impacting the system.

For such things (like, for example, putting a lot of stuff to /tmp, to
avoid quotas), sysadms usually send a message, and lock the accounts if
the owners don't correct it.
 
> 4.  Accounts running password guessers, COPS or TOPS.

Not happened often... A popular 'sport' was putting on false login programs,
which displayed a login/password prompt, and sent them to someone... Nothing
very interesting, I think... (!). This usually leads to lock the account for
a couple of months (there are warnigs all over the lab's walls, telling it).

> 5.  Accounts that are harrassing other users.

The resources are so poor that almost everybody just tries to finish his
project as soon as possible, instead of spending time trying to disturb
other users... So I don't know really, but everything going even a bit
out from a normal use of the account usually leads to a lock.

> 7.  Accounts that breakin to other machines or accounts.

See 2 and 4.

> 8.  Printing of pornographic pictures/gifs.

Everything sent to printers is checked by the operators, who can trash
what they think goes against the rules. Such pictures would never pass.
They usually trash also manuals, mail, and everything that doesn't look
like a program, a project or the final printout of a thesis... They don't
send a messages to inform who sent the 'offending' material to the printer
that he/she couldn't do that. Usually they put out only the first sheet
of the printed stuff, with a notice on it. Who tries to do the print again
has the account locked.
 
> 9.  Other incidents.  Please describe.

The lab policies are rather tough, but the worst problem is that there isn't
a good relationship between users (students) and operators/sysadms. If they
have to ask or tell him something, they usually lock the account, just to
force you to go by them. Then they often re-open it as soon as you come in to
their room. Maybe a simple e-mail message could solve many problems, but they
don't seem to like this way (when I asked why, they told me that people could
always say "well, yes maybe I received the messages, but I can't use the
mailer well to read it..."). This is a very fast way, instead...

The lab had a lot of security problems in the past, I think many more than
the average. In my opinion, a serious cause for it is the lack of a valid
cooperative relationship between students and sysadms. When you suggest
something, in many cases it's rejected; this can happen for several reasons,
but happens most of the times. Everything not regarding projects (students
have temporary accounts only; when they finish projects they loose them) is
strongly discouraged. Hacking grows, but it's not addressed positively
towards useful goals (fixing problems, creating patches, etc.).

Do you sysadms agree about this point? I'd like to hear from you! What pushes
people to try and break systems?

