Charles H. Buchholtz <chip@seas.upenn.edu> wrote:
>Dave Grisham (dave@unm.edu) wrote:
>
>: 2.  Accounts with multiple off-site logins.
>
>[...] But everyone else says, "this isn't unusual", and I realized that 
>this could be interpreted as a series of logins from some locations that
>happen to be off campus. [...] I don't lock.  Why would I lock an account 
>just because the owner is taking a road trip?

Well, I keep a fairly close eye on this one...

I have found that our users often tell friends, former classmates, 
et al about the third-party software that we have installed on our 
systems.  As a result of this and password sharing, I often find 
users coming in from foo.edu to run our software packages.  I'm
all for cooperation, but I don't have the resources to provide sup-
port to users from foo.edu; this is especially true when we're tal-
king about resource-intensive applications such as MATLAB and ANSYS.
In addition, I've had several such incidents which involved .com 
sites; as I understand it, that could be interpreted as a violation 
of our 'educational use only' license agreements, which save us many 
thousands of dollars.   

I've even had incidents in which a local user requested more resources,
which were used (in turn) by his buddy at bleep.edu to conduct some
rather massive ftp sessions.  As it turned out in one case, the user's
buddy could only get 10 Mb of diskspace from bleep.edu, so he saw no 
problem with loaning him some of ours while he made a personal copy
of the UMichigan Mac archive.  Of course, the fellow told me that he 
needed that 25 Mb for his final project. 8(

What do I do?  Well, I routinely run finger and who during the day,
so I have a pretty good idea about who's in the area.  If I see what
I think is an 'account sharing' incident, I'll pop a quick finger to
the remote site; if the names match up, I'll assume that the user has
legitimate access to the remote machine.  If, on the other hand, I
see that "R Bibgo" on the remote machine is logging in as "J Fredrick"
on my system, I'm going to be contacting my counterpart at the remote
site. 

I don't lock accounts for this; a quick email message and face-to-face
explanation of the circumstances (and pointing out that account sharing
is a direct violation of this university's policy) have always been 
enough to get the point across.  

