: Without EFF annotations, etc., please explain to me in your own words why
: you think that a business owner or educational institution should not be
: allowed to regulate what is posted on the net (coming from their site),
: despite the fact that the poster pays for *nothing* (ie: didn't pay for the
: computer and doesn't pay the connect charges).  

	Hmm...  *checks wallet and bank statement* ... I think I spent a
	couple of thousand dollars this year on the University of Toledo.
	I can't really understand where you can regard it as free. 

: Please explain why this
: person has more rights than the person who is providing these services.

	I didn't see Mr. Kadie allege that "this person" had "more rights"
	than the host institution.  To my mind, the host institution is a
	common carrier of Internet information, and, as such, cannot make
	decisions about what it carries based on content.  If it does, then
	it becomes, as editor, responsible for all the content it passes 
	through.  That's why you can't sue AT&T over something someone said
	to you on the phone.  

: Please explain why the person who is posting offensive (to some) material
: or is bad-mouthing a company (the company he works for) product should be
: allowed to continue, despite the fact that everything he is doing is *FREE*.

	Because otherwise the host site becomes responsible for 
	everything that is posted from there.  You can either keep your
	hands off content completely and make content the individual's
	responsibility, or you can assume both the authority to decide 
	and the responsibility for any and all information that is sent
	from your site.  Here, though, I agree with you that in a business
	setting, the employer has every right to regulate on the job 
	conduct and speech in the course of business.  

: He pays for nothing and he doesn't suffer when sales slump because of his
: posts, etc...What gives this person more rights than the equipment owner?

	Nothing, since he has no more rights than the owner, who could, 
	conceivably post messages to counter what "he" says, or could even
	fire him.  Doesn't sound like the owner's got the short end of the
	legal stick, now does it?  

: I'd just like to hear your opinion...Please compare the Internet to something
: like printing up flyers.  Why should the poster be allowed to say whatever
: he wants for free?  Should Kinko's or the university or someone's business
: be responsible for covering the cost of the flyers?  How is that any 
: different than the net?  Should the employee be allowed to use the company
: copy machine to print up material that the company does not approve of?  Does
: the company have *ANY* rights?  Or is this all free speech, the business
: owner be damned?!

	If a public university (for example) extends the right to make free 
	photocopies to one person or group, but withholds it from another
	on the basis that it objects to the content, then it is violating
	the 14th Amendment of the Constitution, which guarantees "equal 
	protection under the law."  This has been decided many times in
	the courts.  

	If a business makes its resources available to one employee for 
	non-company business, then its refusal of the same resources to
	another employee on the basis of the content of his/her message
	would probably be, likewise, unconstitutional, but, since I 
	haven't seen any case citations on that subject, I can't say 
	for sure.  If I, as a business owner, let my employees use company
	copiers and meeting rooms for a European-American employees' group,
	I'd be legally bound to do the same if African-American employees 
	wanted to start their own group.  In short, if the company wants 
	to restrict its resources from being used for personal purposes, 
	it can.  It simply must do so in a fair and even-handed manner. 

