[Carl misses my point about net access and cost effectiveness.]

Carl, 

In an earlier post you mentioned that universities should provide means of
enhancing a student's ability to reach a wider audience as part of their
right of free expression, so long as it was cost effective.  You maintained
that net access had little incremental cost, and so was cost effective,
yet held that the university shouldn't have to provide paper publishing
because that was much more expensive.

I made a case that this was not a tenable argument, as there were ways
in which computer access could be not cost effective.  Whether you realized
it or not, you posted arguments that agreed with this.  

You railed against this as a means of obstructing free speech, but yet
you upheld the "cost-effectiveness" argument about paper publishing.

I merely think your arguments are inconsistent.  If you believe that the
university is in the business of publishing student opinions, then they
have a responsibility to do it on paper, computer, or other media.  If
they are not, then one must acknowledge that the university has control
over all of the media it produces (not the student's right to have an
express an opinion, merely the amount the university will subsidize).

I further believe that rights are orthogonal to cost.  Ezekiel's (and others')
opinions are protected under the first amendment, and should not be
abridged in any form.  However, that does not require anyone to
underwrite the cost of disseminating their views.  If a university 
grants computer access to people, it will inevitably ask that people
behave responsibly with that resource, and is within its right to do so.

					larry...



