If you give me the budget I will hire a security staff to follow every
user 24 hours per day, and ensure that they never tell their password
to anyone else, and never allow somebody else to use their account.

Until I can do that, I am fooling myself if I think that preventing
guessable passwords is a solution to anything.

 [I should have admitted that I have a strong password checker, so that
 users can't set weak passwords in the first place.]

>   >4.  Accounts running password guessers, COPS or TOPS.
>
>           Use a shadow password system, so that this does not matter.
>
>Password files aren't the only thing that COPS looks at, y'know...

So!  I run such software myself from time to time, and attempt to resolve
problems.  But there is always a new user who makes his home directory
publically writeable, and ignores email warnings.  But anyone could find
that much more directly than by running COPS.  There is no practical
way I can determine whether a user is running COPS, and if I should find
a user running it, I cannot read his mind to determine his intentions.
For all I can tell, he might be studying security with the best of
motives.

>   >6.  Accounts that are trying to or have defeated security.
>
>           Find out what they are doing, and tighten security to circumvent
>           the problem.
>
>...and squash them like a grape if it is for deliberate malicious
>purposes.

It is hard to prove "deliberate malicious purposes".  I do put some
commands in groups 'restrict', and not group executable.  Then, if
circumstances warrant, I may add a user to the 'restrict' group,
effectively denying access to those commands.

In a sufficiently serious case I would suspend an account, and require
the user to explain his actions to appropriate people as a condition of
reinstatement.  So far I have not had the misfortune of having to deal
with this problem.  Many of the things that might look like malice turn
out to be cases of stupidity, and in such cases user education is better
than the (virtual) electric chair.

>   >7.  Accounts that breakin to other machines or accounts.
>
>           Warn the administrators of the other machines.  It is up to
>           them to take action.
>
>You've got to be kidding.  That's like calling up your neighbor and 
>saying "Hey, my kid just beat up your kid and rode his motorcycle
>through your garden.  Ha, ha...sucks to be you!"

Well, we are talking abstractions here.  I cannot take action on suspicion.
And I cannot prove that a breakin occurred unless the administrator of
the attacked machine provides evidence.  This is no different than the
cases where the police refuse to arrest someone if you will not file
charges against them.

There was a case on campus a few years ago where a local computer had
a UUCP account which was not password protected, and which listed /bin/sh
as the shell.  (I had not been aware of the security hole.)  I happened
to notice two people logged into that machine from a terminal server I
administer.  A finger told me they were logged in as UUCP.  I logged in
through the same loophole myself, created a '.profile' which would
immediately logged a user out, then I reset the terminal server port to
disconnect the users.  Next I notified the system administrator for the
attacked system, explained what I had done, and suggested security
tightening.

[Please note: my action in the above case was, technically speaking, an
unauthorized intrusion into another system.  Strictly interpreted I
violated the ACM code of ethics.  I personally consider the ACM code of
ethics to be a piece of garbage, since it does not cover realistic
situations.]

The users, in this case, were only breaking in to telnet to MUD sessions,
and fortunately they did no real damage.  One of the users had earlier
logged into a machine I administer from the same terminal server session,
and I was able to track him down and provide him with suitable
re-education.

What more would you want me to do?

>        Don't take all this as a flame, but I think you really need
>to reevaluate some of your sys admin policies.

Hey, I was responding to vaguely specified circumstances.  Without
sufficient information, I assumed mild problems.  You assumed severe
problems.  In the circumstances, why should we agree?

