It should be noted that this is a separate issue from how his system may be
overstressed by users telneting in to run its software.  But many accounts
with ".com" domains are, in fact, individuals' accounts on public access
machines, which happen to be operated by businesses.  Similar services are
sponsored by nonprofit entities, with other domain types (e.g., the Cleveland
Freenet).

Educational software licenses, as I understand it, typically prohibit use
"for commercial purposes."  I doubt that the running of the software by
remote users whose accounts happen to have a .com domain is likely to imperil
the licenses, in and of itself, particularly in light of the stated public
service mission universities in general.

