I don't know whether you've been following the BST debate or
how much you know about BST itself, so here's a summary:

The FDA recently approved the use of BST (bovine
somatotropin) to increase the output of dairy cows.  BST was
developed at Cornell and licensed by Monsanto.  Given the
current state of the dairy industry (a shambles!) and the
amount of money the federal government is paying farmers to
dump milk and destroy cows, a lot of people have questioned
why BST approval went through as quickly as it did,
especially since Canada and Australia have put a moratorium
on the use of BST for 5 years, assuming that more will be
known about its effects on cattle and on humans at that
time.  

It was being discussed in sci.agriculture, so I posted some
facts that I had picked up from the local newspapers and
from a newsletter from the local food coop.  These facts
included which farm and humane organizations were objecting
to the approval of BST, what some of the health hazards to
cattle appeared to be, what the current cost of propping up
the dairy industry is, and the projected costs of the use of
BST would be.  Again, all publicly available information.  I
used *no* abusive language and the articles contained
nothing inflammatory.  I can forward them to you, if you'd
like.  

A few days later I received a telephone call from an
extension agent in [place removed -cmk] thanking me for the
postings and wanting to know if I'd gotten into any trouble.
Apparently he participates in some ag mailing lists run out
of the University of North Carolina (sci.agriculture is
gatewayed into them, which is where he saw my posting).  He
said that some weeks earlier an intern or student employee
at the National Agricultural Library had posted a
bibliography of research papers that had shown negative
effects on cattle from BST use.  Several days later there
was a post from the Head Librarian at NAL apologizing for
the bibliography and letting the recipients know that that
person no longer worked for the library (!!).

I didn't think much of it at the time, but Hung posted something to
the net about some "BST incident" involving me.  I knew nothing about
it and assumed that he was referring to the NAL incident in hopes that
I'd be intimidated by the fact that some employers are firing staffers
for their net activities.  When I spoke with Norm Scott [Cornell's
Vice President for Research and Advanced Studies -cmk] a little while
ago [on a different matter], he said that he had received some
complaints from the USDA about my BST posts.  He didn't go into it any
further, but that's absolutely outrageous.

I would *love* to know what's going on with BST, Monsanto,
the USDA, and the net.

Melinda

