	Speaking as one who has had his account locked without due process, I 
	can give some impressions from a user's perspective:

		1) I was, at first, puzzled why my account was disabled. 
		   I phoned the consulting line, and the consultants there
		   had no idea that my account was disabled, much less why
		   it was disabled.  If you're going to disable an account,
		   at least let the user know why it was disabled.

		2) This being a Saturday, no one would be in at ACS until
		   Monday morning.  Therefore, I was locked out of my 
		   account, without even being able to collect my personal
		   files, for about 48 hours.  Were this real property and
		   not intellectual, the University would have been in a 
		   lot of trouble.  A person's intellectual property should
		   not be denied him without due process.  I believe this is
		   covered under the 4th Amendment. 

		3) On Monday morning, I called Bill Miller, whose name I 
		   had been given to contact about the situation.  After 
		   calling again & again and never having my calls returned, 
		   I camped out on his doorstep (literally) until he returned.
		   He couldn't see me on Monday, but set up an appointment for
		   Tuesday.  This extended my punishment by 24 hours, again 
		   without due process.

		4) Tuesday rolls around and I show up to meet with Mr. Miller.
		   Lo and behold!  I'm now meeting not only with him, but with 
		   Mr. Clifford Collins and Mr. Delbert Waggoner.  I feel that
		   the 3 on 1 approach used on me was, as the Supreme Court once
		   said, "inherently coercive."  This, again, I feel was 
		   contrary to my due process rights. 

		5) Also, having not been apprised of what the meeting was 
