=========================================================================
                GUIDELINES FOR CIVIL SPEECH AND CONDUCT

             Draft report: Subcommittee on Drafting of the
    Committee for the Promotion of Mutual Understanding and Civility
                            February 24, 1993

     Elinor Barber                  Louis Henkin        Michael Paley
     Jean Cohen                     Martha Howell       Marsha Wagner
     Rosalind Fink, Chairperson     Roger Lehecka       Jo-Ann Weissbart
     Eric Garcetti                  Carlton Long

I. CHANGES ON THE CAMPUS.

   Changes in American society and on American university campuses have 
   come in the last twenty-five years with unusual rapidity. Many of 
   these changes are related to newly-recognized or newly-asserted 
   rights based on egalitarian values. Among these changes, the 
   following, perhaps, have the greatest importance for the state of 
   civility in universities like Columbia: (1) the demand by students 
   for a greater voice, made most urgently in the 1960s but continuing 
   to the present and beyond; (2) the vast increase in racial and ethnic 
   diversity of the university population resulting from the civil 
   rights movement, new concerns about equalizing access to education, 
   and an increasing presence of international students and scholars; 
   (3) the women's movement; and (4) the increasing openness of 
   gay/lesbian relationships and new claims for the validity of such 
   relationships.

   In important ways, the assertion of new rights has led to greater 
   emphasis on group affiliation and group identity. Going beyond the 
   universalistic values that have been taken for granted, i.e., the 
   common humanity and dignity of all individuals and equal rights under 
   the law for all individuals, there has emerged a new claim to the 
   right to be different. Members of previously-excluded groups now 
   demand that, regardless of personal differences, they be accorded 
   greater equity, opportunity, and empowerment in a more inclusive and 
   representative society.

   These changes have inescapably altered established relationships 
   between individuals and groups in the society generally and on the 
   campus in particular, creating challenges to traditional authority, 
   changing definitions of acceptable behavior and speech, and 
   stimulating new demands for respect and tolerance. The new kinds of 
   tension and stress on the campus reflect, to an important extent, 
   conflicts among the values underlying the special mission of this 
   university.

II. CHANGING VALUES AND THE MISSION OF THE UNIVERSITY.

   A key component of the mission of Columbia University is the 
   achievement of the highest possible standards in the pursuit and 
   transmission of knowledge. To create an environment in which these 
   activities may flourish and everyone can work and live with dignity, 
   all members of the University community must acknowledge and respect 
   certain core values. These core values include mutual respect for and 
   civility among all individuals and groups on campus; protection of 
   the integrity of each member of the community; and the maintenance of 
   a "free marketplace of ideas," without ideological constraints on 
   instruction, research, or public speaking. Yet acknowledgement of the 
   centrality of these values is not enough; there are settings and 
   situations where they may come into conflict, conflict that has 
   assumed new salience as a consequence of the changes described above. 
   Establishing principles for accommodating the University's core 
   values is the focus of this effort.

   It is important to remember that newly-asserted rights often 
   challenge established rights, and that what once were considered near 
   absolutes are now seen as competing values. For example, the 
   traditional prerogative of professors to teach as they see fit is now 
   challenged by students claiming a right to be spared sexist or racist 
   or homophobic examples or jokes. Members of student organizations who 
   wish to exercise their fully protected right to invite whomever they 
   please to speak on the campus are now challenged by other students 
   who demand respect of their sensitivities. There is new cogency in 
   the admonition to couple the exercise of the right to freedom of 
   speech with the obligation to consider the harm and hurt that 
   exercise of the right may cause others.

   In various ways, then, new tensions and dilemmas attend many of the 
   interactions between individuals and groups at Columbia. In 
   cognizance of the prevailing uncertainties arising from rapid changes 
   in the definitions of civil speech or behavior, there seems to be a 
   place for some guidelines and rules to help define civility. This is 
   not, in the narrow sense, a "speech code," but, rather, an 
   exploration of the following issues: which existing forms of behavior 
   or speech are now considered to be in poor taste or offensive? Which 
   are simply unacceptable? What, if anything, is to be done about them?

III. CIVILITY AND BREACHES OF CIVILITY.

   Civility means respect for rights, but it also means more than that. 
   Civility--courtesy--means sensitivity to the needs, concerns, 
   sensibilities of others; it permits no actions or expressions that 
   purposefully hurt or give offense. Civility means mutual respect, 
   tolerance, an assumption of inherent equality in the face of 
   differences. When members of a community--a university community or 
   any other community--make conscious efforts to understand and respect 
   the values and practices of others who are different from themselves, 
   civility will flourish.

   Incivility, by the same token, means imposing something hurtful or 
   offensive on others. It takes many forms: from littering or excessive 
   noise to tasteless and thoughtless jokes to rudeness, disruption or 
   other aggressive behavior; from various kinds of discrimination and 
   harassment to what is commonly thought of as hate speech or fighting 
   words. All these forms are, to some extent, at issue here, but the 
   focus will be on three of them. One is rudeness; its prevalence, or 
   relative absence, one might say defines the day-to-day level of 
   civility on the campus. A second is harassment, in various 
   manifestations. The third is deliberate stigmatization, words or 
   expression that insult or threaten a member of our community, either 
   as an individual or as a member of a group, because of such specially 
   protected personal attributes as race, ethnicity or national origin, 
   gender, age, religion, disability, or sexual orientation. Deliberate 
   stigmatization occurs infrequently, but each occurrence profoundly 
   offends our concept of civility in the university.

   Just as civility thrives when conscious efforts are made by each of 
   us to understand others, stigmatization tends to be more prevalent 
   when there is unfamiliarity or limited experience with members of 
   different groups. Much of the incivil stigmatization that occurs on 
   the Columbia campus is not deliberate; instead, it results from the 
   kind of ignorance or thoughtlessness that society long condoned and 
   few protested until very recently. Some expressions of 
   stigmatization, while intentional, have as their primary purpose the 
   conveying of an idea; the speaker knows that his or her words or 
   actions may offend individuals or groups, but chooses to take that 
   course because of the perceived importance of the idea or because of 
   a commitment to truth, as the speaker sees it. And, lastly, some 
   words or actions are taken for the primary, or perhaps sole purpose 
   of offending, insulting, or causing pain.

   It is important to note that the very same transgression against 
   civility may arise from ignorance, from thoughtlessness, or from a 
   conscious decision to take the risk of offending in order to convey 
   an important idea, or from the sole intention to injure or insult.

   There clearly are instances in which offense is given without any 
   intent on the part of the offender. One cause of this type of 
   misunderstanding is the new assertion of rights by members of 
   particular groups, a phenomenon explored earlier. But there are other 
   reasons, as well. Columbia is a large, diverse university, bringing 
   together people from very different cultures and different social 
   circumstances. Differences among members of the University 
   community--not just in geographic origin and in racial, ethnic, or 
   religious heritage, but in age, gender, social class, and educational 
   background--make it inevitable that there will be instances where 
   values taken for granted by one group will be alien to another.

   In some cases, unintentional offenses arise, paradoxically, from good 
   intentions: the gesture or comment directed at a person with a 
   disability, intended as friendly or helpful but considered by that 
   person to be patronizing or intrusive; the gesture made in deference 
   to gender or age (stepping aside to let women out of an elevator 
   first, giving a seat to a person of a "certain age" on the subway) 
   that offends the object of the deference. The point may be driven 
   home by noting that, even twenty-five years ago, the terms "sexism" 
   and "homophobia" barely existed. [The rapidity with which particular 
   words can go from preferred, to acceptable, to offensive can be 
   illustrated by the evolution in the terms used to refer to persons 
   with disabilities. In the early 1970's, when this group's civil 
   rights movement began, "crippled" was replaced by "handicapped." By 
   the mid-1980's, "handicapped" was gradually being replaced by 
   "disabled." And recently, both words have become unacceptable. There 
   is now a general consensus that one must speak of "people with 
   disabilities"--earlier usages are becoming a sign of insensitivity, 
   as explicitly recognized in the federal regulations implementing the 
   Americans with Disabilities Act.] In other cases, unintentional 
   offenses arise from a misguided effort to be humorous, as when jokes 
   are made about the compulsive orderliness of Germans or the emotional 
   volatility of Latinos, or from insensitivity, as when African-
   Americans are praised for their athletic skills (with the implication 
   that this is all they are good at) or women are teased about their 
   vanity.

   Ambiguity about intention is also common. Here are some examples.

   * A faculty member presents an interpretation in class that some 
     students resent as racist. In all likelihood, the professor 
     considered the interpretation to be true, fair, and objective, 
     never having considered either the possibility that it could be 
     viewed as racist or that it might be based on prejudiced 
     assumptions. Yet it is also possible that the professor was aware 
     of the potential impact of the interpretation and chose, 
     nonetheless, to present it, either because its pedagogical value 
     outweighed its potential offensiveness, or because of a conscious 
     intention to offend.

   * A white student flies a Confederate flag in her dorm room, perhaps 
     in ignorance of its painful significance for African-American 
     students, or perhaps as a deliberate challenge.

   * A heterosexual student makes negative remarks about gay or lesbian 
     parents. The comments may be made without considering their effect 
     on gays or lesbians who happen to overhear them, or they may be an 
     expression of his own beliefs or dogma, or they may be a deliberate 
     and self-righteous effort to mock the claims of gays and lesbians 
     to the legitimization of their family relationships.

   * A male supervisor is "all hands," frequently touching women who 
     work for him. He may be unaware of their resentment of unwanted 
     physical contact--or he may deliberately insist on his right to 
     engage in this conduct, dismissing their objections to it.

IV. APPROPRIATE RESPONSES TO VARIOUS TYPES OF INCIVILITY

   A. RANGE OF RECOMMENDED RESPONSES

      The main purpose of these guidelines is to state clearly the 
      bounds of civil behavior, in the expectation that members of the 
      Columbia community will conform voluntarily to these precepts. 
      But, situations in which these guidelines are violated, either 
      intentionally or inadvertently, also must be expected. Possible 
      responses to these situations can be placed in four general 
      categories:

      1. PRIVATE, NONDISCIPLINARY RESPONSES--discussion, mediation 
         between the individuals involved, or an educational process 
         aimed at making inadvertent offenders more aware of the 
         damaging effect of their speech or behavior.

      2. USE OF EXISTING DISCIPLINARY MECHANISMS, which could lead to 
         sanction of the offender. Existing disciplinary mechanisms 
         include deans' discipline in the schools; various "progressive 
         discipline" measures governing officers and staff; [See pages 
         52-53 of the Faculty Handbook and Section 75 of the University 
         Statutes (faculty disciplinary mechanisms); pages 70-71 of the 
         Faculty Handbook (officer of research mechanisms); Personnel 
         Policy Manual, Policy 601 (officer of administration, officer 
         of the libraries, and non-union support staff mechanisms); and 
         provisions in each of the 12 collective bargaining agreements 
         covering unionized support staff.] the enforcement provisions 
         in the Rules of University Conduct; and the University-wide 
         Discrimination Grievance Procedure. The range of sanctions 
         against students utilized by these procedures includes verbal 
         or written warning, censure, reprimand, suspension or 
         dismissal. Employee discipline ranges from a verbal warning to 
         a warning letter in a file through suspension and dismissal.

         Most, if not all of the acts of incivility for which the 
         guidelines recommend discipline are already subject to 
         sanction. See, for example, Columbia College's Discipline 
         Policy and the University's Policy Statement on Sexual 
         Harassment.

         It should be emphasized that these guidelines do not recommend 
         a disciplinary response for any speech that would be 
         Constitutionally protected.

      3. TENSION REDUCTION EFFORTS, including public discussions or 
         other programs addressing the incident and its effects. The 
         Working Group for Developing Common Ground, a new unit 
         connected with the Committee for the Promotion of Mutual 
         Understanding and Civility, has been given direct 
         responsibility for efforts to reduce acute group tension and 
         acrimony. It uses such approaches as on-campus discussions, 
         either within student groups or in groups that include faculty 
         and/or administrators; off-campus retreats; mediation and 
         dispute resolution; town meetings; interactive theater; and 
         teach-ins.

      4. ISSUANCE OF A FORMAL, POSSIBLY PUBLIC, STATEMENT. These 
         statements, from the President or the Provost or a dean, should 
         express the University's dismay at a violation of one of its 
         fundamental values. In order to be most effective, they should 
         take into account the circumstances or context of the offense 
         and be carefully tailored to these circumstances; advice from 
         the Ombuds Officer prior to the issuance of the statement might 
         be useful in this regard. In some cases, advice might be sought 
         also from the Working Group for Developing Common Ground or 
         through meetings with the offended and offending groups or 
         individuals. Whether the statement should be public or private 
         would depend on the circumstances. Likewise, it may be 
         appropriate in some cases and not in others for the statement 
         to include suggestions of approaches that would avert a 
         recurrence of the offense or to identify specific University 
         responses to the incident planned by the Common Ground Group, 
         the Ombuds Office, or others.

   B. FACTORS IN DETERMINING APPROPRIATE RESPONSES

      In determining the appropriate response to various types of 
      incivility, six factors (in addition to degree of severity) must 
      be considered: the presence or absence of a speech component; the 
      setting in which the incident occurred; the nature of the action 
      or encounter, i.e. whether or not official University business was 
      being conducted; the intent of the person engaging in the incivil 
      conduct; whether the incident was a one-time occurrence or part of 
      a pattern; and the effect on the victim.

      1. A SPEECH COMPONENT. Some forms of incivility, such as littering 
         or the obstruction of wheelchair access, involve conduct or 
         behavior. They use no words and whatever message they may 
         express is incidental or peripheral. Crafting an appropriate 
         University response to incivility of this form does not involve 
         conflict among core values. Other forms of incivility, such as 
         blockades, involve conduct or behavior that has an expressive 
         component. In these cases, while the speech or expression may 
         be protected, the conduct can be subject to discipline if it 
         violates University regulations, e.g. the Rules of University 
         Conduct. A third category of incivility is speech or expression 
         whose content is incivil. Here, determining the suitable 
         response requires a judgement about whether Constitutionally-
         protected speech values are implicated (in which case 
         discipline is not an option) and about the degree of incivility 
         involved. It is in making these judgments that the other 
         factors of setting, nature of action or encounter, intent, 
         effect, and pattern of offense become important.

      2. SETTING. Setting is defined by both location and function. In 
         academic settings, a very high priority must be given to the 
         free exchange of ideas. These settings include all academic 
         arenas, such as the classroom or the laboratory, when teaching 
         or research is being conducted. Thus, offensive ideas offered 
         in the classroom for pedagogical purposes must be protected, 
         regardless of their impact on some members of the class. But, 
         in contrast, speech in a classroom that is rude, belittling, or 
         intimidating and lacks pedagogical purpose may warrant a 
         response.

         The priority given to the free exchange of ideas in political 
         settings (political arenas like College Walk, or spaces 
         traditionally used by outside speakers, or any other University 
         space, when a speaker is making a public statement on a topic 
         of public interest) is similar to, but not identical with, its 
         priority in academic settings. All political ideas must be 
         tolerated. But if the words or expression used to convey them 
         are highly offensive, a non-disciplinary response may be 
         appropriate. In addition, the Rules of University Conduct might 
         be invoked if the speaker(s) engaged in behavior violating 
         those rules.

         At the other end of the setting spectrum is the non-academic 
         workplace, where the University is not maintaining a "free 
         marketplace of ideas" and is legally obligated to address 
         harassment and discrimination. Moreover, in this setting the 
         University has the right to expect civility, from both 
         employees and students, in the conduct of official University 
         business. Here, the University may sanction incivil speech or 
         expression--primarily if it occurs during the conduct of 
         official University business, but occasionally if it is part of 
         unofficial interactions--that must be tolerated in other 
         settings.

         In the middle of the spectrum are all the other settings on 
         Campus. Physical locations would include hallways, sidewalks 
         between buildings, dining facilities, classrooms or labs when 
         teaching or research is not underway: any setting that doesn't 
         fall within one of the specific categories in which special 
         rules should apply. The response to incivil speech or 
         expression occurring in one of these other settings is 
         determined by the degree of incivility and the other factors of 
         nature of encounter, intent, effect, and pattern of offense.

      3. NATURE OF ACTION OR ENCOUNTER. The University has the right to 
         expect civility from both its students and its employees in the 
         conduct of official University business. It has less legitimate 
         interest in regulating unofficial interactions.

      4. INTENT. Intent is an important factor in gauging the 
         seriousness of any instance of incivility and of the 
         appropriate response. At the same time, there are grey areas 
         between the genuinely innocent or ignorant offense and the 
         indisputably malign one, and any person charged with an act of 
         incivility is free to raise as a defense the claim that the 
         incident was the result of a good faith misunderstanding, 
         rather than an intention to offend.

      5. ONE-TIME OFFENSE OR PATTERN OF INCIVILITY. One relevant factor 
         in determining intent is whether the incivil act is an isolated 
         incident or part of a pattern. An isolated act of incivility 
         might be excused as inadvertent or unintentional. Repetition, 
         however, after offenders have been made aware of the effect of 
         their acts or words on others, can lead to the conclusion that 
         the incivility was deliberate.

         This factor also goes to the severity of the offense. For 
         example, an isolated hateful comment may be protected by free 
         speech values. But a pattern of such comments may constitute 
         harassment, for which University response is both appropriate 
         and, in some cases, legally required.

      6. EFFECT. The seriousness of an instance of incivility depends, 
         also, on its effect on the victim. To a significant extent, 
         incivility is serious if it is so experienced by the victim, 
         and trivial if it is not. It is true that individuals are more 
         and less quick to take offense, and the difficult question 
         whether the victim overreacted should be taken into account.

   C. RECOMMENDED RESPONSES TO ACTS OF INCIVILITY

      This section discusses possible responses to various forms of 
      incivility, from minor incivility through deliberate 
      stigmatization and fighting words. While certain limits are 
      suggested--a recommendation that there be no disciplinary response 
      to acts of truly unintentional incivility, for example--it does 
      not attempt to legislate specific responses to specific acts of 
      incivility. Rather, it identifies a range of possible responses, 
      relying on the individuals to whom acts of incivility have been 
      reported to exercise their judgement and discretion in fashioning 
      an appropriate response.

      1. MINOR INCIVILITY. When relatively minor in degree, the simplest 
         form of response seems appropriate, namely, an exhortation to 
         refrain from such incivility. Thus, members of the University 
         community may be requested not to litter (by signs posted on 
         buildings or by polite suggestions that they deposit their 
         litter in available receptacles) or not to block wheelchair 
         access routes by signs and polite suggestions. Another minor 
         form of deliberate incivility is making excessive noise. 
         Insofar as noise is not already covered by the Rules of 
         University Conduct or regulations applying to fraternities, 
         sororities, and residence halls, those who make excessive noise 
         also may be requested not to disturb others. Persons observing 
         incidents of minor incivility on campus either should confront 
         the individual directly or bring the incident to the attention 
         of nearby security officers. Those who observe repeated acts of 
         minor incivility could report them to such authorities as RAs, 
         department heads, deans, or supervisors, who could resort to a 
         range of responses, including private discussion, an 
         educational process, or institution of disciplinary 
         proceedings.

      2. INCIVILITY THAT IS OR MAY BE UNINTENTIONAL. Genuinely 
         unintentional incivility may warrant some form of private, 
         nondisciplinary response on behalf of the university, either 
         discussion, mediation, or an educational process aimed at 
         making inadvertent offenders more aware of the damaging effect 
         of their speech or behavior. Indeed, unintentional incivility 
         is relatively accessible to remedy just because it is 
         unintentional. If speech, expression, or behavior is not 
         designed to give offense, people of good will, once they have 
         been made aware that they are causing pain to others, will 
         refrain from doing so. The victim of such a genuinely 
         unintentional act may either take no action at all or accept an 
         apology in the spirit in which it is offered.

         But how might the victim of an act of incivility respond when 
         the matter of intention is not so clear-cut? What of the 
         apparently unintentional act? One can easily envision cases in 
         which the offender's disclaimer rings hollow. There are, after 
         all, many people on the campus who have not emerged suddenly 
         from cultural enclaves, who may be presumed to have had at 
         least some exposure to the values of a group to which they do 
         not "belong" and who therefore should have known that their 
         acts or words would cause offense.

         Victims have three choices in this situation--they may still do 
         nothing, they may attempt to deal with the situation themselves 
         by explaining their feelings directly to the person who has 
         caused offense, or they may go to the Ombuds Officer. The 
         Ombuds Officer will offer a range of options indicating, 
         alternatively, ways in which the offense might be handled if 
         the victim chooses either to give the offender the benefit of 
         the doubt, accepting the claim that the offense was 
         inadvertent, or to attribute to the offender the malign 
         intention to harm and hurt.

         If the victim gives the offender the benefit of the doubt, a 
         private, non-disciplinary response probably is appropriate. If 
         the victim chooses to proceed on the claim that the offense was 
         intentional (and if the person hearing the charge agrees), then 
         the response should be governed by the particular form of 
         intentional incivility at issue.

         It should perhaps be stressed that, in all cases where 
         intentional incivility is charged, the person accused is free 
         to argue that there was no malign intention, but rather a good 
         faith misunderstanding between or among the parties. If the 
         individual responsible for dealing with the accusation agrees, 
         then the response to the incident should, in most cases, be 
         private and non-disciplinary.

      3. RUDENESS. We define rudeness as intentionally discourteous, 
         hostile, offensive or vulgar speech or behavior. (A pattern of 
         such rude behavior may constitute harassment, discussed below.) 
         What response, if any, may be appropriate depends on the 
         setting, the relationship of the parties and the nature of the 
         encounter.

         a. OFFICIAL ENCOUNTERS. All members of the University community 
            are entitled to civility in their official dealings with 
            other members of the community. Columbia has a right to 
            demand civil behavior from its faculty and staff when acting 
            in their official capacity. Thus, the response to gratuitous 
            rudeness by an employee while performing his or her work (a 
            receptionist being rude to a student entering an office to 
            make an inquiry; a supervisor speaking rudely to someone on 
            his or her staff; an office manager speaking rudely to 
            someone in Facilities when calling about a problem); or to a 
            faculty member's gratuitous rudeness toward a student in an 
            academic setting should be the same as when a faculty member 
            or other employee's work performance is inadequate in other 
            respects. The person who has been subjected to the rudeness 
            could go to the Ombuds Officer, or make a complaint directly 
            to the rude individual's supervisor or department head, if 
            known.

            Students should be held to the same standard of civil 
            behavior in their official dealings with University 
            employees. Thus, employees subjected to rudeness by students 
            in the course of official dealings (members of the library 
            or bursar's office staff, for example, who are insulted by 
            students while performing their duties), should notify the 
            student's dean, if known, or the Ombuds Officer. Dean's 
            discipline may be appropriate, depending on the situation.

         b. UNOFFICIAL ENCOUNTERS. Individual or occasional instances of 
            rudeness in unofficial encounters (students jeering an 
            athlete who has made a bad play, a co-worker's use of 
            profanity in a personal conversation, for example) should 
            not, ordinarily, be the concern of the University.

      4. HARASSMENT. Harassment involves a pattern of intentional 
         behavior which results in a hostile, intimidating or offensive 
         working or educational environment. It can include a broad 
         range of unprofessional, abusive, or menacing behavior, from 
         repeated belittling, nit-picking, rudeness, hostile pranks or 
         name-calling to meanness, bullying, insults, threats or 
         slander. When the harassment is based on the victim's actual or 
         perceived race, color, religion, national origin, marital 
         status, or gender, the University is legally obligated to 
         address it. [These obligations arise under Titles VI and VII of 
         the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and Title IX of the Education 
         Amendments of 1972. In R.A.V. v. City of St. Paul, Minnesota, 
         504 U.S. __, 112 S. Ct. 2538 (1992), the Supreme Court cited an 
         employer's obligation to prohibit harassment in the workplace 
         under Title VII as one of the exceptions to its prohibition on 
         selective regulation of "fighting words." Using the telephone 
         with intent to harass, annoy, threaten, or alarm another person 
         is also illegal under New York State Penal Law.] Harassment not 
         based on these federally protected categories, however, may be 
         no less obnoxious and has no place on the campus or in our 
         community; whether the harassment occurs in official or 
         unofficial interpersonal encounters, and regardless of its 
         cause, it is unacceptable behavior. Responses on behalf of the 
         university should be designed in ways that both meet the 
         preferences of the individuals involved and put an expeditious 
         end to the harassment. They may run the gamut, therefore, from 
         efforts to resolve the problem privately or to mediate between 
         the parties to initiation of disciplinary proceedings against 
         the offender.

      5. SEXUAL HARASSMENT. Sexual harassment represents a particularly 
         pernicious violation of civility. Following the promulgation of 
         Guidelines on Sexual Harassment by the federal Equal Employment 
         Opportunity Commission, Columbia issued a Policy Statement on 
         Sexual Harassment more than ten years ago. It prohibits sexual 
         harassment of any member of the Columbia community, whether 
         such harassment is aimed at students, faculty, or other 
         employees, and states that violators will be subject to 
         disciplinary action. Sexual harassment is defined as "unwelcome 
         sexual advances, requests for sexual favors, and other verbal 
         or physical conduct of a sexual nature," under two conditions:

         i. If submission to such conduct is made either explicitly or 
            implicitly a term or condition of an individual's employment 
            or used as the basis for academic or employment decisions 
            affecting that individual. This type of harassment involves 
            the abuse of power--coercion by a more powerful member of 
            the University of someone with less power--and includes 
            either an explicit or implicit offer of reward or threat of 
            reprisal.

        ii. If such conduct has the purpose or effect of unreasonably 
            interfering with an individuals' academic or work 
            performance or creating an intimidating, hostile, or 
            offensive academic or working environment. This type of 
            sexual harassment, sometimes called "hostile environment 
            harassment," can occur either between persons in unequal 
            positions of power or between peers. Behaviors that might 
            constitute this type of harassment include sexually 
            exploitative pin-ups in the work place, repeated off-color 
            jokes, unwelcome flirtation, a pattern of leering or 
            standing uncomfortably close to another person, or repeated 
            use of demeaning gender stereotyping. Although the offensive 
            behavior may not be aimed at a single individual, it is 
            considered sexual harassment if its cumulative effect is an 
            adverse impact on an individual's academic or work 
            performance.

         The University's Policy Statement on Sexual Harassment gives 
         the following advice to persons who believe that they are being 
         sexually harassed: as a first step, they should seek a 
         resolution of the problem through discussion with the other 
         person directly concerned. If this does not resolve the matter, 
         or if there is reluctance to deal directly with the person 
         involved, the problem should then be brought to the attention 
         of a member of the University Panel on Sexual Harassment. 
         (Current membership lists are found in all school bulletins and 
         should be posted on bulletin boards throughout the University.) 
         Help also may be sought from the Office of Equal Opportunity 
         and Affirmative Action or the Ombuds Office. If these steps do 
         not resolve the problem, the applicable University grievance 
         procedure should be used, including the University's 
         Discrimination Grievance Procedure that is available if no 
         other University grievance procedure is specifically 
         applicable.

         The University has not barred romantic relationships between 
         faculty and students they are teaching. However, it is always 
         the faculty member's responsibility to interpret with the 
         greatest sensitivity the signals, negative or positive, 
         received from the student and to refrain from exerting any 
         pressure on that student, accepting a refusal without question; 
         not taking "no" for an answer may well constitute sexual 
         harassment.

         Similarly, while employees may "go out" with their supervisors 
         or other persons having authority over them, the University 
         expects these relationships to be developed with extreme 
         caution. Especially if the supervisor initiates the 
         relationship, he or she is expected to take special care to 
         ensure that romantic interest is mutual and to cease all 
         overtures at the first sign that the attraction is not 
         reciprocal. Persistence can quickly cross the line to sexual 
         harassment, if the pursuer has any authority over the person 
         being pursued.

         It is worth noting that romantic relationships even between 
         consenting individuals can be quite troublesome when they 
         involve individuals working in the same office or faculty and 
         students in the same department or school. Confidence in fair 
         treatment of all those working in such an office or department 
         is easily jeopardized by the appearance, let alone the reality, 
         of favoritism between individuals who are romantically 
         involved. It is incumbent on those in such a relationship to 
         make strenuous efforts to ensure that favoritism does not occur 
         nor even seems to occur. Whenever possible, those in the 
         romantic relationship should assign formal assessment of each 
         other's performance to a third party.

      6. DELIBERATE STIGMATIZATION AND FIGHTING WORDS. Two forms of 
         incivil expression are particularly damaging to the University 
         community. One is "fighting words," defined by the Supreme 
         Court as words which "by their very utterance inflict injury or 
         tend to incite an immediate breach of the peace." ["Fighting 
         words" were first declared not worthy of First Amendment 
         protection in Chaplinsky v. New Hampshire, 315 U.S. 568, 572 
         (1942). The "fighting words" exemption was most recently 
         reiterated in R.A.V. v. City of St. Paul, Minnesota, 504 
         U.S.___, 112 S.Ct. 2538 (1992), although the majority in that 
         case held that St. Paul's ordinance violated Fourteenth 
         Amendment equal protection guarantees because it distinguished 
         among classes of fighting words (punishing only those fighting 
         words that insult or provoke violence on the basis of race, 
         color, creed, religion or gender).] Another is deliberate 
         stigmatization, words or expression that insult or threaten a 
         member of our community because of such specially protected 
         personal attributes as race, ethnicity or national origin, 
         gender, age, religion, disability, or sexual orientation. If 
         the deliberate stigmatization is personally directed, it might 
         come within the definition of fighting words. But deliberate 
         stigmatization of a group is also profoundly offensive to all 
         its members and to the community at large.

         For incidents in which the integrity and dignity of individuals 
         is assaulted by these forms of deliberately hurtful speech or 
         expression, the following responses are suggested: [If the 
         incident produced high levels of tension among campus groups, 
         the Working Group for Developing Common Ground might try to 
         design appropriate responses. If the speaker(s) engaged in 
         conduct that violates the Rules of University Conduct, charges 
         might also be brought under those rules.]

         a. FIGHTING WORDS OR DELIBERATE STIGMATIZATION IN ACADEMIC AND 
            POLITICAL SETTINGS. Although, as a private institution, 
            Columbia is not bound by First Amendment law, its mission 
            compels it to recognize the paramount importance of the 
            values reflected in this amendment when dealing with speech 
            or expression in academic or political settings.

            Academic settings include all academic arenas, such as the 
            classroom or the laboratory, when teaching or research is 
            being conducted. Because of the need to protect a free 
            exchange of ideas in academic settings, offensive ideas 
            offered in the classroom for pedagogical purposes should in 
            most cases be protected; speakers, either professors or 
            students, should not fear either discipline or even the 
            issuance of a statement by a university official for 
            expressing their honestly-felt ideas on a subject under 
            discussion in class, regardless of the impact of their ideas 
            on others. By contrast, however, free speech principles 
            should not protect persons using fighting words or 
            deliberate stigmatization in a classroom or laboratory, if 
            the intention of the speaker is not to contribute to a 
            learning or research process, but to offend. In such cases, 
            a range of responses, including institution of disciplinary 
            charges and/or issuance of a formal statement might be 
            appropriate, depending on the circumstances.

            Another aspect of the University's mission requires it to 
            tolerate all political views expressed in political settings 
            (political arenas like College Walk, or spaces traditionally 
            used by outside speakers, or any other University space, 
            when a speaker is making a public statement on a topic of 
            public interest). This very high degree of tolerance, 
            however, while protecting the speaker from discipline, 
            should not preclude the issuance of a formal statement for 
            either fighting words or for deliberate stigmatization, 
            particularly when addressed to individuals or small groups 
            of individuals. [These guidelines do not address the 
            appropriate response, if any, to incivil speech by an 
            outside speaker who has been invited by a campus-based 
            group. This will be addressed in a separate document.]

         b. FIGHTING WORDS OR DELIBERATE STIGMATIZATION IN THE NON-
            ACADEMIC WORKPLACE. The University has the right--if not the 
            obligation--to discipline its employees or students if they 
            use fighting words or deliberately stigmatizing words or 
            expression in the non-academic workplace during the conduct 
            of official University business. Such extreme forms of 
            incivility might also merit issuance of a statement. [Such a 
            statement should be preceded, in most cases, by a quick, 
            preliminary investigation of the incident by the Ombuds 
            Officer.]

         c. FIGHTING WORDS OR DELIBERATE STIGMATIZATION THAT IS REPEATED 
            OR PART OF A PATTERN OF HARASSMENT. Harassment is defined in 
            section IV (C) (4), above, as a pattern of intentional 
            behavior which results in a hostile, intimidating or 
            offensive working or educational environment. Repeated use 
            of fighting words or deliberate stigmatization, or the use 
            of such deliberately offensive speech or expression in 
            conjunction with other harassing behavior, in settings which 
            are neither academic nor political, could lead both to the 
            issuance of a statement and to disciplinary action in 
            accordance with the recommendations in section IV (C) (4).

         d. FIGHTING WORDS OR DELIBERATE STIGMATIZATION IN ALL OTHER 
            SETTINGS. In settings that are neither academic or political 
            nor part of the non-academic workplace, an isolated incident 
            of intentional incivility should not ordinarily be met with 
            a disciplinary response, although the victim might seek 
            university-facilitated discussion, mediation, or other forms 
            of private, non-disciplinary response. Repeated use of 
            fighting words or deliberate stigmatization could lead to 
            issuance of a statement and/or disciplinary action.

V. ROLE OF THE OMBUDS OFFICE

   The Ombuds Office was established to serve as a University-wide 
   resource to assist generally in responding to matters of civility and 
   mutual understanding. Many of its basic functions are especially 
   suited to incivility complaints: confidential counseling and 
   referrals; mediation; educational efforts aimed at making inadvertent 
   offenders more aware of the damaging effects of their speech or 
   behavior; conducting preliminary investigations of incidents; 
   consulting with supervisors on appropriate responses; and reporting 
   to senior administrators on serious civility violations and official 
   misconduct, as well as on patterns of incivility and harassment. The 
   Ombuds Office is also available for consultation by members of the 
   University community who are not sure whether they want to file a 
   formal complaint about an act of incivility, or who are involved in a 
   situation in which a formal complaint is not appropriate, or who are 
   not sure where a formal complaint should be reported (to a dean, a 
   department head, a supervisor, the Office of Equal Opportunity and 
   Affirmative Action, or the Provost or President). If the complaint 
   involves speech or expression which might lead to a formal statement, 
   the Ombuds Office may conduct a preliminary investigation and 
   facilitate communicating the information to the appropriate senior 
   administrator in a timely manner.

VI. ROLE OF THE WORKING GROUP FOR DEVELOPING COMMON GROUND

   The Working Group for Developing Common Ground would have direct 
   responsibility for active involvement in enhancing civility among 
   students, and especially but not exclusively among undergraduate 
   students. This group, which might be called the "Common Ground 
   Group," would try to anticipate and, if possible avert conflicts 
   among groups before they occur. If prevention is not possible, it 
   would make efforts to reduce acute tension and acrimony. The 
   approaches the Common Ground Group would use include on-campus 
   discussion, either within student groups or in groups that include 
   faculty and/or administrators; off-campus retreats; mediation and 
   dispute resolution; town meetings; interactive theater; and teach-
   ins. One objective of the Group would be to stay abreast of emerging 
   intergroup conflicts. The Group would be linked with groups of 
   administrators in Student Services and in Minority Affairs, as well 
   as with Security, the Columbia College Student Council, and the 
   office of the Director of Psychological Counseling in Health 
   Services.

VII. PROTECTION FROM RETALIATION

   It is unacceptable to make any attempt to coerce or intimidate 
   persons bringing a complaint of incivility, or to interfere with the 
   investigative process, or to encourage persons to overlook an offense 
   or retract a complaint. Any such attempt should be brought to the 
   immediate attention of the Ombuds Officer or the senior official in 
   charge.

