suppose we can buy a stapler and some staples), and they gave us extra
money we could never use up in worthless categories like "Postage" to
make it look like they allocated us a lot more than they did.  Then,
to make sure we had our hands tied, they passed a special resolution
on our budget alone to keep us from moving funds from one category to
another (no other group has this restriction).

The hearings were hellish; among other things, they suggested that gay
students should find help from student psychological services, that
our allowing gay candidates for office to make announcements at our
meetings was tantamount to endorsing them (something student groups
aren't allowed to do), that our newsmagazine was "social and
cliqueish" and covered only one issue (apparently, anything having
anything to do with homosexuality was a single issue), and that I had
acted improperly by (in my private life) publicly endorsing a
candidate for the local school board.  Then they passed around signs
we had posted up on our office door (apparently they had torn them
down!) saying "take one!" (most referring to our magazine) and tried to
say that what we were offering were campaign flyers, pornography, and
other things which we have never distributed.

They cut and cut and cut our budget, until I had to resort to a
filibuster (reading the entire text of our budget, word for word)
until the alloted time was up, to keep them from cutting any more.

I'm at wits end with what to do now.  There is no appeals process.
Some people have suggested we cut our ties with our Student Congress
and attempting to go it alone--a dubious possibility at best--but
this strikes me as chickening out.  If student groups like the Black
Student Movement, the Carolina Indian Circle (there are only 131
Native American students on campus, which is easily checked, yet they
received almost four times the money we did, when we have easily
double the membership and ten times the draw) and even the James Bond
Fan Club can receive student funds to an extent where they can
operate, then so should we.  It's the principle of equal rights, after
all.

This is a Student Congress which, two years ago today, hurled epithets
(including "faggot" and "dyke") at the members present for budget
hearings.  Last year, members of Congress illegally obtained our
membership list (our office was broken into with a crowbar) and
distributed it widely (I've received hate mail with copies of the list
from as far away as Oregon and Colorado).  Earlier this year, they
came very close to passing an amendment that would disallow student
funds to go to homosexual groups (they didn't pass it only because the
University attorney warned them that she would dissolve the Congress
if they were to pass this bill which is clearly illegal, since we have
a sexual-orientation nondiscrimination policy).

I would very much like to sue their asses and end this game once and
for all.  One problem.  We have an attorney provided by the
University; however, she is the same attorney that represents the
Congress.  Therefore, we both have to find a third-party attorney to
represent us if we sue the Congress.

Can anyone help me?  I need pointers, suggestions, anything.  I need
to move fast, as this Congress will be replaced by a new Congress in
just two weeks, and we have no claim to sue the next Congress.

I'm very angry and upset at this; we need action, now.

Trey Harris
