In article <jfhCMz2GJ.7qp@netcom.com> jfh@netcom.com (Jack
Hamilton) writes:

>I don't think someone should be censored for their language.

Neither do I, if by censorship you mean prevented from
speaking.  But speech (any form) has consequences, and I have
no problem with holding people responsible for what we say.

I think a person can be held accountable, socially if not
legally, for hate speech.  I don't think there's a way, in a
free society, to get a legal remedy against hate-speakers,
unless they are inciting others to injurious violence.

However difficult it may be to formulate them and get them
disseminated and generally adopted, I think it's acceptable,
even necessary, to have social sanctions that are broadly
accepted in the society as restraints on certain behaviors.
The usual slippery-slope factors apply, of course.

I don't know *how* that could be done (surely it's no snap),
but thinking it's not possible is a step in the wrong
direction, I'm sure.

>I do think that someone should be censored for a pattern of
>inappropriate posting.  By inappropriate posting, I mean
>posts that do not fall within the charter of a newsgroup.

One of the ongoing problems, as I see it, is that we use a
legalistic concept in an environment that doesn't support it
adequately.  Charters do not have the force of law, and even
of they did, there's no way to enforce them in an
unmoderated forum.  Moderation brings on other well-known
problems.  So I think we're left with a big gulf between what
we might want (philosophical and moral issues temporarily to
one side) and what we can get: even if we decide to have
censorship, it couldn't be implemented without profound and
lasting undesirable consequences.

>That includes "Make Money Fast" postings to almost any
>newsgroup,

I thought those were just plain chain letters and therefore
illegal.  Put the law on the perpetrators (no mean feat, I
know).

>and homophobic postings to soc.motss. 

If *only* the denizens there could force themselves to
completely and without exception ignore the phobes, but not
our own homophobia, of course, censorship would not be
needed.  Even milder opprobrium is counterproductive; all it
does is banish the more timid ones and stiffen the resolve
of the more extreme sickos, including the one here present (in
news.admin.misc).

