In <2m5dhd$a2n@polaris.unm.edu> dave@unm.edu (Dave Grisham) writes:

>	I would like to hear from other sys. admins and Univ. computing
>centers what their policy is when locking accounts.

I am a system administrator.  The University has various
disciplinary procedures.  I can refer policy violations to
appropriate disciplinary officers.  University policy does
not allow me to "punish" anyone.  I have had occasion to
refer matters to the police and/or college discipline
committees.  I believe that cutting off someone's access
to resources they need to get their school work done is
beyond my authority.

>1.  Accounts that have a guessable password.

Our student and administrative systems use either shadow
passwords or kerberos so no encrypted strings are exposed.
Since we know the master kerberos password, I supposed we
could expand all the users' passwords into clear text and
look for "bad" ones.  We would have to write something to
do that; we haven't done that.

>2.  Accounts with multiple off-site logins.

If we believe that an account is being used by more than one
person, we modify the login shell so that they are greeted
with a message that invites them to come to our office so
we can change their password. After the message, they get a
shell.  We log that they've received the message and if they
haven't come by within the allotted time (we give them 2 working
days) we lock the account.  When they come to the office, they 
get a printed copy of the rules that includes an explicit 
prohibition against account sharing.  We start timing when 
they first see the message.

>3.  Accounts that have large mail boxes which are impacting the system.

Our student systems get email via a pop reader which immediately
moves their mail to their home directory so it counts against their
quota.  We just implimented a policy which allows us to take
immediate action when e.g. someone gets the entire contents of
a CD-ROM as a mail message.  Our sendmail includes ESMTP extensions
so someday, if this ever gets popular, we may be able to limit
the size of individual messages to something merely unreasonable.

>4.  Accounts running password guessers, COPS or TOPS.

Running a pw guesser is an explicit violation of our rules.
In one case where the person was running it in his own account,
we talked with him and he agreed not to do it again.  In another
case, we called in the police and got the telephone company to
put a number recorder on our entire modem pool.  The person
turned out to be a student.  We refered him to U disciplinary
proceedings and he was suspended for several quarters.

>5.  Accounts that are harrassing other users.

We turn harassment complaints over to the University official that
deals with harassment.  We counsel students claiming to be victims
of harrassment to read the docs that describe the procedures
available to them and to contact U officials that are specialists
in this area.  We act as back end support for the harassment
officer.  We trust the harrassment officer to decide whether
formal disciplinary proceedings are warranted.

>6.  Accounts that are trying to or have defeated security.

Door knob rattling is not a crime.  Absent programs like
crack that require a massive amount of CPU time, it's hard
to tell the difference between  "trying to" defeat security
and door knob rattling.

>7.  Accounts that breakin to other machines or accounts.

If provable, I guess we'd call the police.  

>8.  Printing of pornographic pictures/gifs.

I haven't had this come up.  [We don't run the labs where this
would be an issue]  

>9.  Other incidents.  Please describe.

Abuse of modems.  Sigh.  We've had people who camp on modems
for days at a time, probably running programs at home to beat
our inactivity times.  I've contacted a few people via email
and told them unless they're sitting in front of the screen
using the session that it's a frivolous use of a shared resource.

